       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 2400 mg per dose is three times the usual 800 mg maximum single dose. 9,600 mg/day is three times the 3,200 mg/day ceiling. No standard indication calls for that, and the harm could be serious, including GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and metabolic acidosis. Normal kidney and liver function and no allergies don't change that.\n\nYou can still get the treatment you want ordered:\n- **If 2,400 mg/day was the intent:** I can enter 600 mg PO four times daily or 800 mg PO three times daily now.\n- **If you need more analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect than ibuprofen can give:** Talk with your attending or the clinical pharmacist about alternatives or a multimodal regimen. I can enter those orders once they're decided.\n- **If there is an unusual indication for this dose:** Please discuss it with your attending and pharmacy. If they approve it, it should go through your hospital's override or high-dose approval process, not routine order entry.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
